                <h3><b>Release Scope and Prioritization</b></h3>

                <p>
                    A strong release roadmap clearly defines what will be included in each
                    release and what will remain in the product backlog. Release scope
                    should be based on customer value, strategic objectives, development
                    capacity, technical feasibility, and business urgency.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Group related features and improvements into meaningful release themes.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Prioritize initiatives according to business impact and customer value.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Consider development effort, technical complexity, and dependencies.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Separate committed releases from longer-term roadmap opportunities.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Review priorities regularly as new information becomes available.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="col-lg-4 text-center">
                @* Add the relevant product planning image here when available. *@
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-lg-8" align="justify">
                <h3><b>Release Timeline and Dependency Management</b></h3>

                <p>
                    Release timelines provide teams with visibility into upcoming milestones
                    and delivery expectations. Effective timeline planning considers
                    development, testing, security validation, deployment preparation,
                    stakeholder approvals, and launch activities.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Dependency management is equally important because a single delayed
                    dependency can affect multiple teams and release milestones. Mapping
                    dependencies and assigning ownership helps teams identify blockers early
                    and make informed adjustments to the roadmap.
                </p>

                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Define major development and delivery milestones.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Identify dependencies between teams, systems, and external partners.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Include adequate time for testing, validation, and deployment preparation.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><i class="bi bi-check2-circle color"></i> Track risks and blockers throughout the release lifecycle.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

